<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puduhepa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puduhepa was a Hittite queen of the 13th century BCE, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and the daughter of a priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawazantiya">Lawazantiya</a> in Kizzuwatna. She was one of the most powerful queens of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>: she shared in government, judged cases, reorganised the state cults along Hurrian lines and corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Her seal appears with her husband's on the Hittite copy of the peace treaty with Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puduhepa.jpg" alt="Puduhepa" width="271" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puduhepa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Puduhepa was born at the beginning of the 13th century BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawazantiya">Lawazantiya</a>, where her father Bentepsharri was head priest of the city's goddess Shaushka, identified with the Mesopotamian Ishtar, and she herself served as a priestess of that goddess. Her own tutelary goddess was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebat">Hepat</a>, and her name expressed her devotion to her. According to the account of the marriage, the general Hattusili met her on his return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, and Ishtar told him to find love and companionship in her. She went with him to the kingdom of Hapissa, and although he was probably much older and had many concubines, she became first among them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Hattusili seized the throne from his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhi-teshub">Mursili III</a> in a civil war around 1286 BCE, Puduhepa became tawananna, queen. Her husband was often ill, foreign powers were slow to recognise him and the deposed king Urhi‑<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a> was still at large, so her help mattered. She stood beside him as he gave rulings, had her own seal, ran the royal palaces and judged court cases, and she was shown ruling hand in hand with him rather than beneath him. She also identified the Sun goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebat">Hepat</a> in the reorganised pantheon.</p><p>From the rebuilt capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> she used her children to secure alliances, a role no Hittite queen had played before. Letters under her seal passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> during the peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, under which two of Hattusili's daughters went to marry the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>; one daughter became Queen Maathorneferure of Egypt, another queen of Isuwa. When Ramesses scorned a Babylonian marriage, she replied that if he said the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> was not a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>, he did not know Babylon's status. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a> of Egypt also wrote to her and sent gifts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Hattusili's death her role grew, at least early in the reign of her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a>, under the title of goddess-queen. A later coup saw her expelled from the palace by her daughter-in-law; some scholars think she was later restored. She may have lived to ninety, or perhaps only seventy-five or eighty. She has been called one of the most influential women known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puduḫepa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 13th century BCE, the queen and priestess <b>Puduhepa</b> worked on organising and rationalising her people's religion.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its cults passed to the Hittite court; Queen <b>Puduhepa</b> was a priest's daughter from Lawazantiya.</blockquote><a href="/state/kizzuwatna">Kizzuwatna</a></li></ul></section>
